                <pb facs="0476" n="462" sameAs="frus:frus1949v05/pg462"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>At his news conference on February 10, President Truman stated that Secretary of
                    State Acheson’s statement regarding the trial of Cardinal Mindszenty (page 461)
                    had his entire approval. The President characterized the court that tried the
                    Cardinal a “kangaroo court”. He observed that a police state existed in Hungary,
                    and the Hungarian people were not responsible for the actions of their
                    government. He also said that the possibility that the trial had been a
                    violation of the Hungarian Peace Treaty was being studied. For text of the news
                    conference, see <hi rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the Presidents of the
                        United States: Harry S. Truman, 1949</hi> (Washington, Government Printing
                    Office, 1964) pages 130–133.</p>
            </div>
